From rolf at math.unb.ca  Wed Mar 27 16:08:58 2002
From: rolf at math.unb.ca (Rolf Turner)
Date: Wed, 27 Mar 2002 12:08:58 -0400
Subject: RSS in step() output.
Message-ID: <200203271608.MAA12193@gelfand.math.unb.ca>

A colleague (who does not subscribe to this list) has asked me to
enquire of the list how the column ``RSS'' in the output of step() is
calculated, for generalized linear (explicitly, logistic binomial)
models.  (She wants to be able to explain to a consulting client, in
complete detail, what every item in the output means.)

E.g.:

out4 <- glm(y~x1+x2+x3,family=binomial)
step(out4,direction="backward")

       Df Sum of Sq      RSS       Cp 
<none>              34932.66 34940.66  OK
    x1  1   4.84595 34937.50 34943.50 !! No idea !!
    x2  1  40.85799 34973.51 34979.51
    x3  1  41.83639 34974.49 34980.49

The entries in the ``Sum of Sq'' column appear to be the difference
between the corresponding RSS value and the RSS value for ``none''.

For an ordinary garden-variety linear model, the RSS values
would simply be the residual sum of squares for the corresponding
model.  E.g. the RSS value for the ``x1 row'' would be the
residual sum of squares for the model y ~ x2 + x3.

How are the RSS values calculated in the case of a glm?  We gather
that the appropriate residuals to use are the Pearson residuals.
We also gather that an approximation is used (instead of refitting
and calculating residual sums of squares for each new model, which
would be very time consuming).

However, everything that we've tried by way of calculating the
RSS values gives results which are ***very*** different from
the RSS column which appears above.  (Making it implausible that
the given RSS value is an approximation to our ``exact''
calculations.)

Can someone please tell us how to

        (a) calculate exact values for the RSS column,

and, if possible,

        (d) do the approximation explicitly so as reproduce
        the RSS column (as given by step()) exactly?

NOTE:  What we want to be able to do is, say, to form

        fit1 <- glm(y~x1+x2+x3,family=binomial)
        fit2 <- glm(y~x2+x3,family=binomial)

and then, by doing ``simple'' calculations on the components
of fit1 and fit2, reproduce the RSS value for the x1 row.

Any enlightenment would be much appreciated.

                                cheers,

                                        Rolf Turner
                                        rolf at math.unb.ca

