If I fit a model like:

> fit1 <- lm( y ~ x1 + x2 + x3 + x4, data=my.data )

Then I can do a stepwise regression like:

> step(fit1, scope=list(lower= ~1, upper= .^3))

and it will test for interactions, but only test the x1:x2 interaction if
both x1 and x2 are currently in the model.

I would like to have this same behaviour, but also to test terms like x1^2
and x1^3.

if I use I(x1^2), etc. in the formula statements then it will try putting
them in, but it does not keep to the heirarchical principle of only trying
to add x1^2 if x1 is currently in the model and only adding x1^3 if x1 and
x1^2 are already in the model.

I can also use poly(x1, x2, x3, x4, 3) to generate all of the variables
that I am interested in testing, but then step will not test the
individual columns, only the matrix as a whole (or I can use the result
with the y variable as the new dataset but each term is considered at each
step without caring about which other terms are in the model).

It seems like we should be able to just adjust the terms object to show
that my I(x1^2) term is of order 2 and dependent on x1, but my attempts
along that line have failed so far.

Does anyone have any code or suggestions for including the polynomial
terms in a stepwise regression.

