From Michael.Wing at orst.edu  Thu Mar 28 22:48:07 2002
From: Michael.Wing at orst.edu (Wing, Michael)
Date: Thu, 28 Mar 2002 14:48:07 -0800
Subject: Summary: Regression Tree Significance
Message-ID: <6B677297622ED311A8E100902785F9310302A722@ribes.for.orst.edu>

Special thanks to Tim Hesterberg, Steve Hogan, Sean Keenan, and D. Mckenzie for all providing helpful input regarding my latest challenge with regression trees.

My original post:

>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>

Rejwan and others (Ecology 80(1):341-348, 1999) demonstrate a method for examining the statistical significance of regression trees. They examine significance by randomly ordering the dependent variable and rerunning the tree function 1000 times. A distribution of explained variances is created from this process and the initial explained variance (from the original pruned tree) is compared to the distribution to see if it is among the top 5%. I'd like to run a similar test and have been unsuccessful in my attempts to automate this process. Can anyone provide guidance on how to accomplish this in SP 2000 in a Windows environment?

>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>

Almost all responses recommended that I look into bootstrapping and this did end up providing a solution.  Tim Hesterberg was particularly helpful in providing code to help me along.

The successful code:

lglwdv11 <- fish2$LGLWDV1 # The dependent variable as a vector object for random ordering purposes

lglwdv11.bootobject <- bootstrap(data = "" statistic = rsquared(prune.tree(tree(lglwdv11 ~ GEO + GRADIENT + CONSTRAN + ACW + LANDUSE1 + OWNER2, data = "" na.action = "" minsize=20),best = 8)), B = 1000, sampler = samp.permute, assign.frame1=T)

The bootstrap function above references the dependent variable vector (ldlwdv11) for each of the 1000 random permutations while reading the independent variables from a data frame (fish2).  It generates a distribution of 1000 explained variances that result from the nested rsquared function.  The last two components of the bootstrap call were not default settings but were important for my purposes.  The samp.permute avoided the default Monte Carlo simulation and the assign.frame ensured that an updated set of the randomly ordered dependent variable was used for each tree run. 

Thanks again to all of you who provided help.

Michael Wing

