From kimmerer at sfsu.edu  Sat Mar 30 19:02:55 2002
From: kimmerer at sfsu.edu (Wim Kimmerer)
Date: Sat, 30 Mar 2002 11:02:55 -0800
Subject: Help!! Trying to migrate from Splus 2000 to v. 6 Release 2
Message-ID: <3.0.6.32.20020330110255.00b00990@sfsu.edu>

Hello, I am trying to migrate from Splus 2000 to v. 6, against my better
judgment (I fiercely resist most "upgrades" as a waste of time).  I looked
through the archives for answers to my questions, and did not find anything
other than a random collection of problems reported, some of which I have
experienced.  I rarely use the GUI, so have not got into some of the
difficulties reported there.

Just for starters: I had finished installing Splus 6 and a colleague came
in with a data-analysis question.  So I cut and pasted two small blocks of
data using read.table, then used chron to convert the first column to dates
data type for graphing.  Then the program quit working (details below).
There was not a shred of information anywhere in Help or any of the manuals
that hinted at anything I had done wrong.  I also noticed that the program
seemed a lot slower than Splus 2000 at doing ordinary things like opening
graph windows or loading libraries.  So, I had to ask myself whether this
was worth the bother.  Subsequent messing with it did nothing to convince
me that it was.

First, my configuration:

Win98, 1G PIII with 256 MB and 30 GB

I have been experiencing numerous apparently unrelated problems trying to
do this.  Below are a few examples, which I have sent to Insightful and
have received responses for some.  I am less interested in the specifics of
these problems, since experience to date suggests there will be numerous
other problems with other specifics. 
-----------------------------------
WHat I would like to know from those with experience in this conversion is
the following:

1.      Are the "improvements" to Splus in v. 6 worth the hassles associated
with this migration?  I gather memory management is one such, but the
archives contain difficulties with that too.

2.      Has anybody compiled a list of the problems encountered and their
solutions?

3.      Has anybody compiled a list of the functions that no longer work as
advertised, and that are not listed as defunct?  (One example I have
encountered, see below, is "chron")

4.      Has anybody written a manual for this program (as it stands) as opposed
to some other, related but not really compatible program (as Insightful has
done).

-------------------------------
Some examples, all of which indicate bugs rather than problems I should be
aware of and solve myself:

1.      The migrate "Wizard" is dumb as a box of rocks and plain doesn't work.
The command line conversion (and just TRY to find "migrate" in Help!)
function convertOldLibrary worked OK, but crashed several times when trying
to convert functions.

2.      The "dates" data type is apparently obsolete, but nowhere is this
information presented.  Trying to use data frames containing dates data
types results in a conversion of an existing data frame to something that
is no longer seen by the program as a data frame at all.  It looks as if I
will have to write a function to go through every one of my existing data
sets and convert all "dates" data to timeDate, if that is possible.
convertOldLibrary doesn't do this.  

A question to Insightful yielded the following:

We encourage switching to the new timeDate/timeSeries/timeSpan
functions.....
These functions have supplanted the dates() function and are described
in Chapter 25 in the S-PLUS 6 Guide to Statistics, Volume 2.

This is helpful as far as it goes, but NOWHERE in the referenced chapter is
there any statement to the effect that the chron function will give you
data that the program subsequently can't interpret. 

3.      After the above error occurred, on plotting the data that I thought was
in a suitable format (it plotted, too!), the program just quit responding
to commands.  In either the command window or a script window, all I got
was a line feed, with messages at the bottom such as:

Problem: Invalid S-PLUS object
Problem: Object coercion failure: data.frame
and sometimes:
Problem: Failed to evaluate call

This was reported before in the archives with no solution listed.  I have
not yet heard back from Insightful about this.

In addition, after plotting the data frame referred to above, I found that
the data frame had been changed to a list, even though the "plot" command
does nothing to the data it uses!  And after that happened, on trying to
open a completely different data frame, the program crashed with the
following message:
  SPLUS caused an exception c0000026H in module KERNEL32.DLL at 017f:bff88539.
etc.

4.      I created a new chapter called Functions, then converted the old
functions from Splus2000 using the command convertOldLibrary and put them
in Functions.  If I attach that chapter I should then have access to my
functions.  Accepting that some may not work any more, it should give me
problems only when I try to run those functions.  Instead, as soon as I
attach the chapter at position 2, I can't access anything:

> x     # This is an existing data frame in my main directory:
Problem in close: argument number 1 in call not matched: close(file) 
Use traceback() to see the call stack
> q()   # I can't quit the program from the command line
Problem in close: argument number 1 in call not matched: close(file) 
Use traceback() to see the call stack
> traceback()   # And I can't do what the program just said I could.
Problem in close: argument number 1 in call not matched: close(file) 
Use traceback() to see the call stack

--------------------------
Any help appreciated, but especially on the four basic questions!

Wim Kimmerer

